From f2346ec45a54064e911555e725501b36dd90931b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 07:49:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] docs(widgets): the Android timerInterval equivalent,
 investigated and costed

The owner asked whether Android has something like iOS's Text(timerInterval:),
accepting a format change if it keeps the widget correct. It does: a
Chronometer with setChronometerCountDown, redrawn by the launcher's own
process with zero app wakeups and immune to every alarm policy.

It is not built, for three verified reasons. expo-widgets' converter handles
14 view types and has no case for Chronometer, TextClock or
AndroidRemoteViews, falling through to "View not found". A partial update
cannot reach into a Glance tree because Glance owns the view ids. So it would
mean replacing the generated providers and rebuilding all eight cards in
RemoteViews XML, abandoning the shared layout pipeline.

And the gain is narrow: a Chronometer does not tick while the screen is off
either, it re-syncs when it becomes visible, so its only real advantage over
the shipped TIME_TICK fix is the sub-minute window that fix already closes.
Measured on the Find X8: 38% exact before, 9 of 9 exact after.

A WidgetCountdownOverlay was written against the partial-update assumption and
deleted once dumpsys showed there is no view id of ours to target.
