+# The Android equivalent of iOS's `timerInterval`: what it is, and what it would cost
+
+Branch: `experiment/25-chronometer-ticking`. Nothing here ships. This file answers the owner's
+question, asked 2026-09-26: *"on the iPhone we are using a timer interval, which is the only way to
+keep the app up to date. Perhaps on Android we have something similar or different, which might change
+the layout of the timer, which is okay."*
+
+The answer is yes, there is an equivalent, and it is worth knowing exactly what it buys and what it
+costs, because the cost turned out to be much higher than the iOS change was.
+
+## The mechanism
+
+| | iOS | Android |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| API | `Text(timerInterval:)` | `Chronometer` + `RemoteViews.setChronometerCountDown(id, true)` |
+| Who redraws it | iOS, in its own process | the LAUNCHER, in its process (`postDelayed`, `Chronometer.mTicker`) |
+| App wakeups needed | none | none |
+| Can it be deferred by doze, standby or an OEM policy | no | no |
+| Text it can render | `6:02:00` | `6:02:00`, or `MM:SS` under an hour |
+| Text it cannot render | `6h 2m` | `6h 2m` |
+
+So the parallel is exact, including the format loss. `Chronometer` substitutes only `%s` for the whole
+timer string; there is no pattern language for unit suffixes. `TextClock` is the other self-ticking
+`@RemoteView`, but it renders the CURRENT time through a `SimpleDateFormat` pattern, not a countdown,
+so it cannot express "time until Dhuhr" at all.
+
+Verified against the resolved artifacts rather than from memory:
+
+- `EmittableAndroidRemoteViews` and 14 translator references exist in the peek fork
+  (`glance-appwidget-1.2.0-peek-0.3.0.aar`), so Glance itself CAN host raw RemoteViews.
+- `RemoteViews.setChronometer` (API 1) and `setChronometerCountDown` (API 24) are both available at
+  this module's `minSdk` 24.
+
+## Why it is not a small change here, unlike on iOS
+
+On iOS the switch was a layout edit: one `Text` swapped for another inside a SwiftUI tree we already
+control. On Android three separate walls stand in the way, each verified:
+
+1. **expo-widgets cannot emit it.** `ExpoWidgetEmittableTree.kt` converts exactly 14 view types
+   (`BoxView`, `ColumnView`, `RowView`, `TextView`, `ImageView`, `SpacerView`, the progress and
+   control views, and five Button flavours). There is no case for `Chronometer`, `TextClock` or
+   `AndroidRemoteViews`, and an unknown element name falls through to
+   `else -> listOf(createErrorText("View not found"))`. So it cannot be reached from the JSX layout at
+   all, at any cost, without patching the library, which the owner has ruled out.
+
+2. **A partial update cannot target it.** The obvious cheap route is to let Glance draw the card and
+   then overlay a Chronometer with `partiallyUpdateAppWidget`. That needs a stable view id inside the
+   composed tree, and Glance assigns its own ids at composition time. `dumpsys appwidget` shows only
+   `views=android.widget.RemoteViews@d09174c` with nothing we own. An overlay was written against this
+   assumption and then deleted, because it cannot work.
+
+3. **So it means abandoning Glance for the affected kinds.** The generated providers are trivial
+   (`class PrayerWidgetProvider : ExpoWidgetsAppWidgetProvider("PrayerWidget")`), so they COULD be
+   replaced with hand-written `AppWidgetProvider`s that build the whole card in RemoteViews XML. That
+   means re-implementing, in XML and Kotlin, per theme and per size: the card background, the hero
+   trio, the day list with its active pill, the moon drawables, the footer, and the proportional
+   column sizing from session 15d. Eight kinds. The entire Android widget layout pipeline would stop
+   being shared with iOS.
+
+## What it would actually buy, measured
+
+This is the part that decided it. The Chronometer's advantage over the shipped fix is narrower than it
+first appears, because **a Chronometer does not tick while the screen is off either**. It re-syncs from
+its `elapsedRealtime` base when it becomes visible. Its real gain is therefore only the sub-minute
+staleness window, and the shipped `TIME_TICK` fix already closes that:
+
+| | 1.28.21, before | 1.28.23, after the TIME_TICK fix |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Exact readings | 38% | **100%** (9 of 9) |
+| Worst drift | +2 min | **+0** |
+| Label flips after the edge by | 13 to 40 s | ~1 to 4 s |
+
+The one case the Chronometer would still cover and `TIME_TICK` cannot is **the app process being dead
+outright**, where no receiver is registered to redraw anything. Against that, the Chronometer is
+genuinely better, and if long-idle sampling ever shows that case dominating real use, this is the
+answer and this file is the starting point.
+
+## Recommendation
+
+**Do not build it.** Ship the `TIME_TICK` registration fix, which costs nothing, keeps `6h 2m`, and
+measures exact. Keep this document as the designed fallback.
+
+If the owner wants it anyway, the smallest honest version is worth considering: apply it to the two
+SMALL kinds only, whose cards are a name, a countdown and a time, and leave the mediums on Glance. That
+bounds the rewrite to something like a day's work instead of a week's, and it would read `5:12:00`
+where the medium beside it reads `5h 12m`, which is a visible inconsistency the owner should see on
+device before choosing.
