+## The mechanism, isolated to the second
+
+A live experiment on 2026-09-26 with the screen kept ON, watching one minute pass, separates the two
+failure modes and shows which one is actually producing the owner's drift.
+
+| Device clock | Widget showed | True (ceil) | Drift | What had happened |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 07:25:10 | `5h 34m` | `5h 32m` | **+2** | the 07:25 edge had passed; that minute's render had NOT run yet |
+| 07:25:19.314 | | | | the alarm finally fires, 19.3s late, and the widget redraws |
+| 07:25:36 | `5h 32m` | `5h 32m` | **+0** | correct again |
+
+So the widget is not frozen and the data is not stale. **The error is a staleness WINDOW that opens at
+every minute edge and closes when the late alarm finally fires.** Inside that window the widget is
+showing the previous minute's value, which reads as "+1 minute". If a fire lands so late that it
+crosses the following edge, two edges collapse into one render and the window reads "+2".
+
+Measured lateness of our alarm across seven fires: 16.7, 21.6, 27.9, 23.2, 13.0, 34.4, 19.3 seconds.
+
+- Average: **22.3 s**, worst: **34.4 s**.
+- So roughly **37% of the time**, a glance at the widget shows a number one minute too high.
+- That matches the sampled table exactly: mostly 0 or +1, occasionally +2.
+
+### Delivery is NOT the blocker while the screen is on
+
+An earlier reading of `widget is not ready` / `host.callbacks: null` suggested the launcher could never
+be updated. Re-measured properly, it is screen-state dependent:
+
+| Screen | Deferred (`widget is not ready`) | Delivered (`updateViews:`) |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| OFF, 105 s window | 13 | **0** |
+| ON, same app | 0 | **15** |
+
+Both matter, and they matter in different ways:
+
+1. **Screen ON:** delivery works. The only error is the late-fire staleness window above. This is the
+   case the user actually sees, because a user looking at their home screen has the screen on.
+2. **Screen OFF:** nothing can be delivered at all, because the launcher has torn down its host
+   callbacks. **No app-side mechanism can fix this, and none needs to: nobody is looking.** What matters
+   is that the value is correct by the time the user looks, and the launcher re-inflates all three
+   widgets on wake (`AppWidgetHostView: inflateAsync(rvToApply), appWidgetId: 53/56/57`).
+
+This is why `ACTION_TIME_TICK` is the right lever and the alarm is not. The system broadcasts TIME_TICK
+at the edge with `windowLength 0`, so a redraw driven by it lands at `:00` instead of `:22`, which
+closes the staleness window. It only fires while the screen is on, which is precisely when it is needed.
+
